04:57:23,430 --> 04:59:23,030
Sen. Francis “Chiz” Escudero: Section 1, Rule 129 of the Rules Provide, a court shall take judicial notice without need of the introduction of evidence of legislative, executive, and judicial departments of the National Government of the Philippines. As stated in CLT, the case cited by the counsel for the prosecutors, there is such, referring to a Senate Committee report, The court took judicial notice of the Senate committee report but only as to the existence of the committee report and not as to the content and truth of what was stated in the committee report. Therefore, accordingly, the court grants the requested judicial notice to be taken by the court but solely and exclusively limited to the following facts. Number one, that the legislative hearings were actually held. and under the official function of the House of Representatives Committee on Good Government and Public Accountability and the Committee on Justice. So to reiterate, just as to the factual existence that the hearings were indeed conducted. Number two, that the corresponding transcripts, official audiovisual recordings and committee report exist. As official records of the proceedings and in a separate rule, therefore enjoy the regularity of official proceedings absent contrary proof that there is either fraud, falsity, or inaccuracy, therefore which will overcome the presumption of regularity. With a warning. Having granted the motion, only within the foregoing limits, the chair now states in unmistakable terms the precise effect of this ruling and reminds the prosecution, and should the time come, also the respondent, if they happen to cross this line also, Rule

1434
04:59:24,760 --> 05:01:00,150
Sen. Francis “Chiz” Escudero: 129 is captioned, What need not be proved. Therefore, the court states with precision what the prosecution need not prove, only with respect to those two things. Nothing else is judicially admitted or established. The prosecution is therefore expressly reminded that judicial notice shall not be used as a vehicle to place into the record substantive contents of the transcripts, recordings, or committee reports. It is not a substitute for the presentation and formal offer of competent evidence, nor does it dispense with the applicable rules on authentication, hearsay, relevance, and the right of the respondent to object and confront the evidence presented against her. As the Supreme Court held in Magkamit v. IAS, PIDEA, GR No. 19-8-140-125-16, fair play requires. that the respondent be given meaningful opportunity to rebut all the evidence presented against her. Therefore, consistent with the Chair's prior ruling on July 21 in relation to the Las Cañas document, a witness will not hereafter be allowed to read in open court a third-party statement that is not his or her own, as the respondent would be denied the opportunity to rebut those statements and cross-examine. The statements made by a third party.

